 ],
 "literal": "House-dwelling fowl's short-legged hen / because evening has approached with a wildcat on the fence, / not knowing where to enter, gathering together and huddling, / like calling out to its distressed flock of chicks, / with unpleasant sounding gossip, / do not come, may you live long, O lord, to our street.",
 "translation": "O Lord, may you live long! Do not come to our street accompanied by the unpleasant gossip of the town, which sounds like the cries of a short-legged house hen calling out to her distressed flock of chicks, huddling together in fear, not knowing where to go when evening falls and a wildcat appears on the fence.",
 "modern": "வீட்டில் வாழ்கின்ற குட்டைக் கால்களைக் கொண்ட பெண் கோழியானது, வேலியின் மேல் காட்டுப்பூனை வந்த மாலைப் பொழுதில், புகலிடம் தெரியாமல் அஞ்சித் தவித்து, தன் துன்புறும் குஞ்சுகளை ஒன்றுசேரக் கூவி அழைப்பது போல, இவ்வூர் மக்கள் அலர் தூற்றுகின்றனர். ஐயனே! நீவிர் வாழ்க! இத்தகைய இன்னாத அலர் மொழிகளோடு எமது தெருவிற்கு இனி வர வேண்டா.",
 "explanation": "The companion (தோழி) speaks to the hero (தலைவன்) who has been visiting the heroine secretly at night. She tells him to stop coming to their street because his secret visits have triggered loud, unpleasant gossip (அம்பல்) among the townspeople. She compares this gossip to the loud, distressed cries of a mother hen calling out to her chicks when a wildcat (வெருகு) appears on the fence at dusk. The helpless chicks represent the heroine and her family, who are terrified of the damage the wildcat (the scandal) will do to their reputation. This domestic imagery belongs to the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, highlighting the social pressure on the lovers to formalize their union through marriage.",
